  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A sprawling area of high pressure will reside over \nmuch of the Eastern U.S. through the upcoming week. Days will be\nwarming each day, but overnight lows will continue below normal\nwith freezing temps possible each morning through mid week. \nBlended guidance struggles to capture lows in good radiational \nsetups like this, and lows were dropped below blended guidance \nthrough mid week to account for this. The coldest nights look \nto be Monday night and Tuesday night. Despite many areas seeing \nfrost/freeze impacts, the program has been ended for much of ENC\ndue to the widespread freeze that occurred Sunday morning. In \nlight of that, any frost/freeze headlines will be confined to \nDowneast Carteret, Pamlico, Hyde, and Outer Banks Dare \ncounties. Frost and freeze headlines may be needed for these\nmainland zones Monday night/Tuesday morning. \n\nLow-level thicknesses will steadily warm mid to late week, and even \nthough high pressure will remain in place, temps will warm to\nabove climo with 70s inland. For the OBX, highs will be \ntempered by light northeast breezes off the cooler waters of \nthe Labrador Current as water temps have cooled to the upper\n50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A very dry airmass remaining in place over the \ncoming week will exacerbate the worsening drought conditions across \nENC. Fortunately winds are not expected to be strong with the high \nnearby. However, fire weather concerns may grow with the curing \nvegetation combined with low RH values each afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...There is some potential for a coastal low to \ngain strength off the FL coast late in the week. While there is \nmuch uncertainty on where the system tracks, the last several \nmodel cycles of the AIFS, GFS and GFS AI have indicated a \nnortheastward track off the Southeast US coast. Spread regarding\nlow pres location remains on the 15/12Z AIFS ensembles this far\nout in time, though many members do forecast a strengthening \nlow off the coastline this weekend. We will continue to monitor \nthe system, as large waves and other coastal impacts could be \nrealized if this system materializes.",
